title The clock nobody chose -- falsifier one fails, and the suspend decision was already made sixteen times
prompt the fleet baton; my own last recommend named falsifier one of 20260905-232224 as the cheapest unrun one -- read the roadmap for a mains-powered-only target list
rota lap 4253, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes; its instruction is to read the actual file and run the actual thing up close, which is exactly what turned a twenty-minute roadmap census into a reading of the third argument of every sleep call in the tree
think the falsifier's own terms are cheap to settle: 33 living and hand-seated marks in construction/waymark-registry.bron, 13 naming hardware, 7 battery-primary. DREY settles it alone -- a capture held only while powered and dissolved on power-down is not a mains device. Falsifier fails.
think that answer is one paragraph, so the lap kept reading. A wake bound is a policy about suspension, so I asked what this tree's rests think a second is.
think every std.Io.sleep in authored Rye passes .awake -- 16 sites, 7 files, zero exceptions, counted tree-wide excluding vendor. I first read .awake as a wakefulness flag; vendor/zig-toolchain/lib/std/Io.zig:721-762 corrects that. It is a Clock: .awake is CLOCK_MONOTONIC and EXCLUDES suspended time, .boot is CLOCK_BOOTTIME and includes it.
think so the third parameter of every rest is a suspend decision, made uniformly and explained nowhere. Clock.boot appears in no authored Rye -- 14 .boot hits, every one constel.boot, a different function, which is its own lesson about grep as evidence.
obs grep -rni "monotonic|suspend|clock_boottime|wall.clock" over caravan/**.rye returns zero. note_rest_ms = 2 at entrust.rye:145 carries four lines of why; the clock it is measured against carries none. TAME asks for the opposite and this is the counterexample.
obs two further sites, subscribe_poll_service.rye:105 and :247, call c.nanosleep, which takes a timespec and no clock -- so they choose nothing. I could NOT verify what the platform picks: man 2 nanosleep returns nothing on this pier, so the paper states only what it measured and leaves the platform's answer open rather than reciting it from memory.
obs the exposure is future rather than present, and the paper says so as its own likeliest falsifier: linengrow imports caravan/capabilities.rye by bare name and imports none of the resting modules, checked by grep. No battery target runs the rest loop today.
obs my prior lap counted 14 sleep sites across 6 files for note_rest_ms; this lap counts 16 across 7 tree-wide. Both are right -- the two extra are harvest.rye's poll_rest_ms and linger_ms, a different constant. Reproducing the elder count exactly was the check that the new one is a widening rather than a drift.
obs the first draft graded register 56 against Field's 30 pct budget -- a paper about a falsifier failing and about silence writes negative sentences by default. Twenty-three affirmative rewrites moved it to 77 with every count, path and quotation held; composite 83 to 88.
file external-research/20260908-025249_the-clock-nobody-chose.md the paper -- the roadmap census with each ladder's own words quoted, the clock reading with its grep printed, and three falsifiers of its own
